@author "Claude (Anthropic)"

; Fisher's waterbomb base through both layers in one statement, the variant
; his syntax cannot tell apart from flatten-waterbomb-top-layer.bel
; [fisher1994, §5.3]. The creases are marked on both layers, and one fan item
; with `up to` folds both layers under the table point of .n as one stack
; (spec multifold, "A fan with up to folds every layer down to a flap"): one fan
; at the point of each layer, with the same rays. The rays name their pieces
; in both layers: --ab and --cd each hold an edge of both layers, and .s0 and
; .s1 lie on the spine. `(staying .b .c)` names the sector along the raw edges
; by the two corners of the top layer that lie on its bounding rays
; (spec multifold, "staying names the part that holds every point"); the bottom
; layer's face in that sector stays with it. The bottom layer faces down, so
; each letter folds it the other way round on the paper, and the two layers
; keep their order.

paper rectangle 2 1
fold (map .a onto .b) (moving .b) as --spine
.s0 = --spine * --ab
.s1 = free on --spine from .s0 at 1
mark (through .a .s1) as --d1
mark (through .s0 .d) as --d2
mark (map .a onto .s0) as --v
.n = (--d1 & .b) * --d2
.e = free on --ab from .a at 1/4
.f = free on --ab from .a at 3/4
flatten (fan at .n (--d1 & --ab valley) (--d1 & .s1 valley)
                   (--d2 & .s0 valley) (--d2 & --cd valley)
                   (--v & --ab mountain) (--v & --cd mountain)
                   (up to #[.a]))
        (staying .b .c)

; assert steps = 2
; assert faces = 12
; assert .a = (0, 0)
; assert .d = (0, 1)
; assert .b = (0, 0)
; assert .c = (0, 1)
; assert .n = (1/2, 1/2)
; assert .s0 = (0, 0)
; assert .s1 = (0, 1)
; assert .e = (0, 1/2)
; assert .f = (0, 1/2)
